ge cash edge, and raters overwhelmingly call the race Likely R. Osborn's independent bid and polling near even keep this from Safe R, but the leaked audio controversy and outside GOP spending push the expected margin toward the fundamentals.", "watch": "Whether the leaked-audio damage fades or additional polling shows Osborn consolidating independents could move this back toward a genuine tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Pete Ricketts (R) is running.", "Dan Osborn is an independent standing in for the Democratic side."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's Senate race pits appointed incumbent Pete Ricketts against independent Dan Osborn, who is running as the Democratic-side challenger after a surprisingly competitive 2024 bid. The state's ten-point Republican lean and Ricketts's fundraising advantage set a high bar, yet polls through mid-September show a surprisingly tight contest, with averages near even and one recent survey giving Ricketts a modest lead.\n\nThe race's tone turned sharply after leaked recordings showed Osborn making blunt, profane remarks about some Trump voters, drawing attacks from Ricketts and a $1.4 million GOP super PAC ad buy. Prediction markets price Ricketts as a heavy favorite, and most expert raters call the race Likely R, with a couple at Lean R.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1905, "margin": -4.44}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1913, "margin": -5.0}]}